# Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) Architecture Rules

## Overview

This project follows **Feature-Sliced Design (FSD)** methodology for organizing frontend code. FSD is an architectural methodology that structures code by business features rather than technical layers, promoting scalability, maintainability, and clear boundaries.

## FSD Layers (Bottom to Top)

```
app/          # Application initialization, routing, providers
├── pages/    # Full-page compositions
├── widgets/  # Complex UI blocks (optional, use when needed)
├── features/ # User interactions, business actions
├── entities/ # Business entities (optional, use when needed)
└── shared/   # Reusable infrastructure code
```

### Import Rules (Dependency Direction)

**CRITICAL**: Imports can only go **UPWARD** in the layer hierarchy. Lower layers cannot import from higher layers.

```
app → pages → widgets → features → entities → shared
 ↑      ↑        ↑         ↑          ↑         ↑
Allowed import direction (upward only)
```

**✅ CORRECT:**
- `features/auth` can import from `shared/components/ui`, `shared/lib`
- `pages/dashboard` can import from `features/auth`, `shared/components/ui`
- `app` can import from all layers

**❌ WRONG:**
- `shared/components/ui` importing from `features/auth` (violates dependency rule)
- `entities/user` importing from `features/profile` (violates dependency rule)
- `features` importing from `pages` (violates dependency rule)

## Layer Definitions & Rules

### 1. `shared/` - Reusable Infrastructure

**Purpose**: Foundation layer containing reusable code with no business logic.

**Contains:**
- UI primitives (buttons, inputs, cards, etc.)
- Utility functions (`lib/utils.ts`, formatters, validators)
- Hooks without business logic (`use-debounce`, `use-media-query`)
- Types and constants (if truly shared across multiple features)
- API clients and configurations
- Theme providers, context providers

**Rules:**
- ❌ NO business logic
- ❌ NO feature-specific code
- ❌ NO imports from `features/`, `entities/`, `pages/`, `app/`
- ✅ Can import from external libraries only
- ✅ Must be framework-agnostic when possible

**Structure:**
```
shared/
├── components/
│   └── ui/          # UI primitives (Shadcn components)
│       ├── button.tsx
│       ├── input.tsx
│       ├── card.tsx
│       └── ...      # All Shadcn UI components
├── lib/             # Utility functions
│   ├── utils.ts
│   ├── format.ts
│   └── validators.ts
├── hooks/           # Reusable hooks (no business logic)
│   ├── use-debounce.ts
│   └── use-media-query.ts
├── api/             # API clients, axios instances
│   └── client.ts
├── config/          # App-wide configurations
│   └── constants.ts
└── types/           # Shared TypeScript types
    └── index.ts
```

### Using Shadcn UI Components

**Shadcn UI components are located in `@/shared/components/ui/`**. These are base UI primitives that should be used as building blocks for feature-specific components.

**✅ DO:**
- Import Shadcn components from `@/shared/components/ui/`
- Compose Shadcn components to create feature-specific UI
- Extend Shadcn components with additional props when needed
- Use Shadcn components as foundation for custom components

**❌ DON'T:**
- Modify Shadcn components directly in `shared/components/ui/`
- Duplicate Shadcn component logic in features
- Create feature-specific versions of Shadcn components without composing them

**Example - Using Shadcn Components:**
```tsx
// ✅ features/auth/ui/sign-in-form.tsx
'use client';

import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { Input } from '@/shared/components/ui/input';
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';
import { useSignIn } from '../hooks/use-sign-in';

export function SignInForm() {
  const { mutate: signIn, isPending } = useSignIn();

  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>Sign In</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
          <Input name="email" type="email" placeholder="Email" />
          <Input name="password" type="password" placeholder="Password" />
          <Button type="submit" disabled={isPending}>
            {isPending ? 'Signing in...' : 'Sign In'}
          </Button>
        </form>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

**Example - Composing Shadcn Components:**
```tsx
// ✅ features/products/ui/product-card.tsx
'use client';

import { Card, CardContent, CardFooter, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { Badge } from '@/shared/components/ui/badge';
import { Avatar, AvatarImage, AvatarFallback } from '@/shared/components/ui/avatar';
import type { Product } from '../model/types';

interface ProductCardProps {
  product: Product;
  onAddToCart?: (productId: string) => void;
}

export function ProductCard({ product, onAddToCart }: ProductCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <Avatar>
          <AvatarImage src={product.image} alt={product.name} />
          <AvatarFallback>{product.name[0]}</AvatarFallback>
        </Avatar>
        <CardTitle>{product.name}</CardTitle>
        <Badge variant="secondary">{product.category}</Badge>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <p className="text-2xl font-bold">${product.price}</p>
        <p className="text-sm text-muted-foreground">{product.description}</p>
      </CardContent>
      <CardFooter>
        <Button onClick={() => onAddToCart?.(product.id)}>
          Add to Cart
        </Button>
      </CardFooter>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

**Example - Extending Shadcn Components:**
```tsx
// ✅ features/forms/ui/form-input.tsx
'use client';

import { Input } from '@/shared/components/ui/input';
import { Label } from '@/shared/components/ui/label';
import { ComponentProps } from 'react';
import { cn } from '@/shared/lib/utils';

interface FormInputProps extends ComponentProps<typeof Input> {
  label?: string;
  error?: string;
  helperText?: string;
}

export function FormInput({ label, error, helperText, className, id, ...props }: FormInputProps) {
  const inputId = id || `input-${Math.random().toString(36).substr(2, 9)}`;

  return (
    <div className="space-y-2">
      {label && (
        <Label htmlFor={inputId} className={error ? 'text-destructive' : ''}>
          {label}
        </Label>
      )}
      <Input
        id={inputId}
        className={cn(error && 'border-destructive focus-visible:ring-destructive', className)}
        aria-invalid={!!error}
        aria-describedby={error ? `${inputId}-error` : undefined}
        {...props}
      />
      {error && (
        <p id={`${inputId}-error`} className="text-sm text-destructive">
          {error}
        </p>
      )}
      {helperText && !error && (
        <p className="text-sm text-muted-foreground">{helperText}</p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 2. `entities/` - Business Entities (Optional)

**Purpose**: Business entities that represent core domain models. Use when you have entities shared across multiple features.

**Contains:**
- Entity models (User, Product, Order)
- Entity-specific UI components
- Entity-specific hooks and utilities
- Entity types and schemas

**When to use:**
- Entity is used by 3+ features
- Entity has complex business logic
- Entity needs to be reused across different contexts

**Rules:**
- ❌ NO feature-specific logic
- ❌ NO imports from `features/`, `pages/`, `app/`
- ✅ Can import from `shared/` only
- ✅ Can import from other `entities/` (carefully)

**Structure:**
```
entities/
├── user/
│   ├── model/
│   │   └── types.ts
│   ├── ui/
│   │   └── user-card.tsx
│   └── api/
│       └── user-api.ts
└── product/
    ├── model/
    │   └── types.ts
    └── ui/
        └── product-card.tsx
```

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ entities/user/model/types.ts
export interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  avatar?: string;
}

// ✅ entities/user/ui/user-card.tsx
import { User } from '../model/types';
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';

export function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>{user.name}</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <p>{user.email}</p>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

### 3. `features/` - User Interactions & Business Actions

**Purpose**: Self-contained features representing user interactions and business actions.

**Contains:**
- Feature-specific components
- Feature-specific hooks (business logic)
- Feature-specific API calls
- Feature-specific stores (Zustand, etc.)
- Feature-specific types and schemas
- Server actions (Next.js)

**Rules:**
- ❌ NO imports from `pages/`, `widgets/`, `app/`
- ✅ Can import from `shared/`, `entities/`, other `features/` (via public API only)
- ✅ Each feature must have a public API (`index.ts`)
- ✅ Feature should be self-contained and independently testable

**Structure:**
```
features/
├── auth/
│   ├── index.ts              # Public API (exports only)
│   ├── ui/
│   │   ├── sign-in-form.tsx
│   │   └── sign-up-form.tsx
│   ├── model/
│   │   ├── types.ts
│   │   └── schemas.ts        # Zod schemas
│   ├── api/
│   │   └── auth-api.ts
│   ├── hooks/
│   │   ├── use-sign-in.ts
│   │   └── use-sign-out.ts
│   └── lib/
│       └── auth-helpers.ts
├── product-list/
│   ├── index.ts
│   ├── ui/
│   │   └── product-list.tsx
│   └── hooks/
│       └── use-product-list.ts
└── cart/
    ├── index.ts
    ├── ui/
    │   └── cart-button.tsx
    └── store/
        └── cart-store.ts
```

**Public API Pattern:**
```tsx
// ✅ features/auth/index.ts
// Export only what other features/pages need
export { SignInForm, SignUpForm } from './ui';
export { useSignIn, useSignOut } from './hooks';
export type { AuthUser } from './model/types';

// ❌ DON'T export internal utilities, internal components
```

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ features/auth/ui/sign-in-form.tsx
'use client';

import { useSignIn } from '../hooks/use-sign-in';
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { Input } from '@/shared/components/ui/input';
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';
import { signInSchema } from '../model/schemas';

export function SignInForm() {
  const { mutate: signIn, isPending } = useSignIn();

  const handleSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const formData = new FormData(e.currentTarget);
    const data = signInSchema.parse({
      email: formData.get('email'),
      password: formData.get('password'),
    });
    signIn(data);
  };

  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>Sign In</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
          <Input name="email" type="email" placeholder="Email" />
          <Input name="password" type="password" placeholder="Password" />
          <Button type="submit" disabled={isPending}>
            {isPending ? 'Signing in...' : 'Sign In'}
          </Button>
        </form>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

### 4. `widgets/` - Complex UI Blocks (Optional)

**Purpose**: Complex, reusable UI blocks that combine multiple features/entities. Use sparingly.

**Contains:**
- Composite components combining multiple features
- Complex layouts
- Dashboard sections, headers with multiple features

**When to use:**
- Component uses 2+ features
- Component is a major UI block (header, sidebar, dashboard section)
- Component is reused across multiple pages

**Rules:**
- ❌ NO imports from `pages/`, `app/`
- ✅ Can import from `features/`, `entities/`, `shared/`
- ✅ Should compose features, not duplicate their logic

**Structure:**
```
widgets/
├── header/
│   ├── index.ts
│   └── ui/
│       └── header.tsx
└── dashboard-stats/
    ├── index.ts
    └── ui/
        └── dashboard-stats.tsx
```

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ widgets/header/ui/header.tsx
'use client';

import { UserNav } from '@/features/user-profile/ui/user-nav';
import { CartButton } from '@/features/cart/ui/cart-button';
import { Input } from '@/shared/components/ui/input';
import { Search } from 'lucide-react';

export function Header() {
  return (
    <header className="flex items-center gap-4 p-4 border-b">
      <div className="relative flex-1 max-w-md">
        <Search className="absolute left-2 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 h-4 w-4 text-muted-foreground" />
        <Input placeholder="Search..." className="pl-8" />
      </div>
      <CartButton />
      <UserNav />
    </header>
  );
}
```

### 5. `pages/` - Full Page Compositions

**Purpose**: Full-page components that compose widgets and features into complete pages.

**Contains:**
- Page-level components
- Page-specific layouts
- Route-specific logic

**Rules:**
- ❌ NO imports from `app/`
- ✅ Can import from `widgets/`, `features/`, `entities/`, `shared/`
- ✅ Should be thin - delegate logic to features/widgets
- ✅ Use Next.js App Router patterns (Server/Client Components)

**Structure:**
```
pages/
├── dashboard/
│   ├── index.ts
│   └── ui/
│       └── dashboard-page.tsx
└── product-detail/
    ├── index.ts
    └── ui/
        └── product-detail-page.tsx
```

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ pages/dashboard/ui/dashboard-page.tsx
import { Header } from '@/widgets/header';
import { DashboardStats } from '@/widgets/dashboard-stats';
import { RecentProducts } from '@/features/product-list/ui/recent-products';

export function DashboardPage() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Header />
      <DashboardStats />
      <RecentProducts />
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 6. `app/` - Application Layer (Next.js App Router)

**Purpose**: Next.js App Router directory - routing, layouts, providers, metadata.

**Contains:**
- Route files (`page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`)
- API routes (`api/`)
- Root layout and providers
- Route groups
- Metadata configurations

**Rules:**
- ✅ Can import from all layers
- ✅ Should be minimal - delegate to `pages/`
- ✅ Follow Next.js 16 App Router conventions
- ✅ Use Server Components by default, add `'use client'` only when needed

**Structure:**
```
app/
├── layout.tsx              # Root layout
├── page.tsx                # Home page
├── (auth)/
│   ├── sign-in/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   └── sign-up/
│       └── page.tsx
├── (dashboard)/
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   ├── dashboard/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   └── products/
│       └── page.tsx
└── api/
    └── auth/
        └── route.ts
```

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ app/(dashboard)/dashboard/page.tsx
import { DashboardPage } from '@/pages/dashboard';

export default function DashboardRoute() {
  return <DashboardPage />;
}

// ✅ app/(dashboard)/dashboard/page.tsx (with data fetching)
import { DashboardPage } from '@/pages/dashboard';
import { getDashboardData } from '@/features/dashboard/api/dashboard-api';

export default async function DashboardRoute() {
  const data = await getDashboardData();
  return <DashboardPage initialData={data} />;
}
```

## File Naming Conventions

### Components
- Use `kebab-case.tsx` for component files
- Component name should match file name (PascalCase)
- Example: `user-profile-card.tsx` → `UserProfileCard`

### Hooks
- Prefix with `use-`: `use-auth.ts`, `use-product-list.ts`
- Hook name: `useAuth`, `useProductList`

### Types
- Use `kebab-case.types.ts`: `user.types.ts`, `product.types.ts`
- Or co-locate with component: `user-card.tsx` + `user-card.types.ts`

### Utilities
- Use `kebab-case.ts`: `format-date.ts`, `validate-email.ts`

### Folders
- Use `kebab-case` for folder names
- Segment folders: `ui/`, `model/`, `api/`, `hooks/`, `lib/`, `store/`

## Public API Pattern (index.ts)

**Every feature/entity/widget must have a public API:**

```tsx
// ✅ features/auth/index.ts
// Export public interface only
export { SignInForm, SignUpForm } from './ui';
export { useSignIn, useSignOut, useAuth } from './hooks';
export type { AuthUser, SignInCredentials } from './model/types';
export { signInSchema, signUpSchema } from './model/schemas';

// ❌ DON'T export:
// - Internal utilities
// - Internal components
// - Implementation details
```

**Usage:**
```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT: Import from public API
import { SignInForm, useAuth } from '@/features/auth';

// ❌ WRONG: Import from internal path
import { SignInForm } from '@/features/auth/ui/sign-in-form';
```

## React Clean Code Best Practices

### 1. Component Structure

**✅ DO:**
- Keep components small and focused (Single Responsibility)
- Use descriptive, meaningful names
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Use TypeScript for all components
- Prefer composition over inheritance

**❌ DON'T:**
- Create god components (doing too much)
- Mix business logic with UI rendering
- Use `any` type
- Create deeply nested components without extracting

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Small, focused component
export function UserAvatar({ user, size = 'md' }: UserAvatarProps) {
  return (
    <div className={cn('avatar', size)}>
      <img src={user.avatar} alt={user.name} />
    </div>
  );
}

// ❌ BAD: Component doing too much
export function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => {
        setUser(data);
        setLoading(false);
      })
      .catch(err => {
        setError(err);
        setLoading(false);
      });
  }, [userId]);
  
  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
  if (error) return <div>Error: {error.message}</div>;
  
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{user.name}</h1>
      <p>{user.email}</p>
      {/* ... 100 more lines */}
    </div>
  );
}

// ✅ GOOD: Extracted logic to hook
export function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { data: user, isLoading, error } = useUser(userId);
  
  if (isLoading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage error={error} />;
  
  return <UserProfileContent user={user} />;
}
```

### 2. Custom Hooks

**✅ DO:**
- Extract reusable logic into hooks
- Return objects/arrays with named values
- Use descriptive hook names starting with `use`
- Handle loading, error states in hooks
- Keep hooks focused on single responsibility

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Focused hook with clear return
export function useUser(userId: string) {
  const { data, isLoading, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['user', userId],
    queryFn: () => userApi.getUser(userId),
  });

  return {
    user: data,
    isLoading,
    error,
    isError: !!error,
  };
}

// ✅ GOOD: Hook with mutations
export function useUpdateUser() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();

  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: userApi.updateUser,
    onSuccess: (data) => {
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['user', data.id] });
    },
  });
}
```

### 3. TypeScript Best Practices

**✅ DO:**
- Define types for all props
- Use `interface` for object shapes, `type` for unions/intersections
- Export types from feature's `model/types.ts`
- Use utility types (`ComponentProps`, `Pick`, `Omit`)
- Avoid `any`, use `unknown` if type is truly unknown

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Proper typing
interface UserCardProps {
  user: User;
  onEdit?: (user: User) => void;
  className?: string;
}

export function UserCard({ user, onEdit, className }: UserCardProps) {
  // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD: Extending component props
import { ComponentProps } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';

type CustomButtonProps = ComponentProps<typeof Button> & {
  isLoading?: boolean;
};

export function CustomButton({ isLoading, children, ...props }: CustomButtonProps) {
  return (
    <Button disabled={isLoading} {...props}>
      {isLoading ? 'Loading...' : children}
    </Button>
  );
}
```

### 4. State Management

**✅ DO:**
- Use React Query for server state
- Use Zustand for client state (when needed)
- Use `useState` for local component state
- Lift state up only when necessary
- Keep state as close to usage as possible

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Server state with React Query
export function useProducts(filters?: ProductFilters) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['products', filters],
    queryFn: () => productApi.getProducts(filters),
    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
  });
}

// ✅ GOOD: Client state with Zustand (when needed)
export const useCartStore = create<CartState>((set) => ({
  items: [],
  addItem: (item) => set((state) => ({ items: [...state.items, item] })),
  removeItem: (id) => set((state) => ({ items: state.items.filter(i => i.id !== id) })),
}));
```

### 5. Performance Optimization

**✅ DO:**
- Use Server Components by default (Next.js)
- Add `'use client'` only when needed (hooks, interactivity, browser APIs)
- Use `React.memo` for expensive components (sparingly)
- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` only when necessary
- Code split with dynamic imports for large components

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Server Component (default)
import { ProductList } from '@/features/products/ui/product-list';

export default async function ProductsPage() {
  const products = await getProducts();
  return <ProductList products={products} />;
}

// ✅ GOOD: Client Component (when needed)
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';

export function InteractiveButton() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <Button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</Button>;
}

// ✅ GOOD: Dynamic import for code splitting
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

const HeavyChart = dynamic(() => import('@/features/analytics/ui/chart'), {
  loading: () => <ChartSkeleton />,
  ssr: false,
});
```

### 6. Error Handling

**✅ DO:**
- Handle errors at appropriate boundaries
- Use error boundaries for component errors
- Handle API errors in hooks/services
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Log errors appropriately

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Error handling in hook
export function useUser(userId: string) {
  const { data, error, isLoading } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['user', userId],
    queryFn: () => userApi.getUser(userId),
    retry: 1,
    onError: (error) => {
      console.error('Failed to fetch user:', error);
      toast.error('Failed to load user data');
    },
  });

  return { user: data, error, isLoading };
}

// ✅ GOOD: Error boundary
'use client';

import { ErrorBoundary } from 'react-error-boundary';

function ErrorFallback({ error }: { error: Error }) {
  return (
    <div role="alert">
      <h2>Something went wrong:</h2>
      <pre>{error.message}</pre>
    </div>
  );
}

export function App() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}>
      <YourApp />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
```

### 7. Code Organization

**✅ DO:**
- Group related code together
- Use consistent file structure within features
- Keep files focused (one main export per file when possible)
- Use barrel exports (`index.ts`) for public API
- Organize imports: external → internal, grouped by type

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Organized imports
// External libraries
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';

// Shared code
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { cn } from '@/shared/lib/utils';

// Feature code
import { useAuth } from '@/features/auth';
import { UserCard } from '@/entities/user/ui/user-card';

// Types
import type { User } from '@/entities/user/model/types';
```

### 8. Testing Considerations

**✅ DO:**
- Write testable code (pure functions, dependency injection)
- Keep business logic separate from UI
- Use dependency injection for API calls
- Make components easily testable

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD: Testable hook with dependency injection
export function useUser(userId: string, api = userApi) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['user', userId],
    queryFn: () => api.getUser(userId),
  });
}

// ✅ GOOD: Testable component
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';

export function UserCard({ user, onEdit }: UserCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>{user.name}</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        {onEdit && <Button onClick={() => onEdit(user)}>Edit</Button>}
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

## Next.js 16 App Router Specific Rules

### Server vs Client Components

**✅ Use Server Components by default:**
- Data fetching
- Accessing backend resources
- Keeping sensitive information on server
- Large dependencies (reduce client bundle)

**✅ Use Client Components when:**
- Using hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, etc.)
- Using browser APIs (`window`, `localStorage`, etc.)
- Event listeners (`onClick`, `onChange`, etc.)
- State management libraries (Zustand, etc.)
- Animation libraries (Framer Motion, etc.)

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ Server Component (default)
import { getProducts } from '@/features/products/api/product-api';

export default async function ProductsPage() {
  const products = await getProducts();
  return <ProductList products={products} />;
}

// ✅ Client Component (when needed)
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';

export function ProductFilters() {
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState({});
  // ... interactive logic
}
```

### Data Fetching

**✅ DO:**
- Fetch data in Server Components when possible
- Use React Query for client-side data fetching
- Use Server Actions for mutations
- Handle loading and error states

**Example:**
```tsx
// ✅ Server Component data fetching
export default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const product = await getProduct(params.id);
  if (!product) notFound();
  return <ProductDetail product={product} />;
}

// ✅ Server Action
'use server';

import { revalidatePath } from 'next/cache';

export async function updateProduct(formData: FormData) {
  const id = formData.get('id') as string;
  const data = Object.fromEntries(formData);
  await productApi.updateProduct(id, data);
  revalidatePath(`/products/${id}`);
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Feature with API, Hooks, and UI

```
features/product-list/
├── index.ts                 # Public API
├── ui/
│   └── product-list.tsx
├── hooks/
│   └── use-product-list.ts
├── api/
│   └── product-api.ts
├── model/
│   ├── types.ts
│   └── schemas.ts
└── lib/
    └── product-helpers.ts
```

### Feature with Store

```
features/cart/
├── index.ts
├── ui/
│   └── cart-button.tsx
├── store/
│   └── cart-store.ts
└── model/
    └── types.ts
```

## Checklist Before Committing

- [ ] All imports follow FSD dependency rules (upward only)
- [ ] Feature has public API (`index.ts`) with proper exports
- [ ] No business logic in `shared/` layer
- [ ] Components are small and focused
- [ ] Types are properly defined
- [ ] Server/Client Components are used correctly
- [ ] Error handling is implemented
- [ ] Code follows naming conventions
- [ ] No circular dependencies
- [ ] Public API doesn't expose internal implementation

## Migration Guide (If Refactoring)

1. **Identify features**: Group related functionality
2. **Move to `features/`**: Extract feature code
3. **Create public API**: Add `index.ts` with exports
4. **Move shared code**: Identify truly shared code → `shared/`
5. **Update imports**: Use public APIs, fix dependency violations
6. **Test**: Ensure everything still works

## Shadcn UI Components Quick Reference

### Import Path
All Shadcn UI components are located in:
```
@/shared/components/ui/{component-name}
```

### Common Components
```tsx
// Buttons & Actions
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { Badge } from '@/shared/components/ui/badge';

// Forms
import { Input } from '@/shared/components/ui/input';
import { Label } from '@/shared/components/ui/label';
import { Textarea } from '@/shared/components/ui/textarea';
import { Checkbox } from '@/shared/components/ui/checkbox';
import { RadioGroup, RadioGroupItem } from '@/shared/components/ui/radio-group';
import { Select, SelectContent, SelectItem, SelectTrigger, SelectValue } from '@/shared/components/ui/select';
import { Switch } from '@/shared/components/ui/switch';
import { Slider } from '@/shared/components/ui/slider';

// Layout
import { Card, CardContent, CardDescription, CardFooter, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';
import { Separator } from '@/shared/components/ui/separator';
import { Tabs, TabsContent, TabsList, TabsTrigger } from '@/shared/components/ui/tabs';

// Overlays
import { Dialog, DialogContent, DialogDescription, DialogHeader, DialogTitle, DialogTrigger } from '@/shared/components/ui/dialog';
import { Sheet, SheetContent, SheetDescription, SheetHeader, SheetTitle, SheetTrigger } from '@/shared/components/ui/sheet';
import { Popover, PopoverContent, PopoverTrigger } from '@/shared/components/ui/popover';
import { DropdownMenu, DropdownMenuContent, DropdownMenuItem, DropdownMenuTrigger } from '@/shared/components/ui/dropdown-menu';

// Feedback
import { Alert, AlertDescription, AlertTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/alert';
import { AlertDialog, AlertDialogAction, AlertDialogCancel, AlertDialogContent, AlertDialogDescription, AlertDialogFooter, AlertDialogHeader, AlertDialogTitle, AlertDialogTrigger } from '@/shared/components/ui/alert-dialog';
import { toast } from 'sonner'; // from '@/shared/components/ui/sonner'

// Data Display
import { Table, TableBody, TableCaption, TableCell, TableHead, TableHeader, TableRow } from '@/shared/components/ui/table';
import { Avatar, AvatarFallback, AvatarImage } from '@/shared/components/ui/avatar';
import { Skeleton } from '@/shared/components/ui/skeleton';

// Navigation
import { Breadcrumb, BreadcrumbItem, BreadcrumbLink, BreadcrumbList, BreadcrumbPage, BreadcrumbSeparator } from '@/shared/components/ui/breadcrumb';
```

### Usage Pattern
```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT: Compose Shadcn components in features
// features/products/ui/product-card.tsx
'use client';

import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from '@/shared/components/ui/card';
import { Button } from '@/shared/components/ui/button';
import { Badge } from '@/shared/components/ui/badge';

export function ProductCard({ product }: { product: Product }) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>{product.name}</CardTitle>
        <Badge>{product.category}</Badge>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <Button>Add to Cart</Button>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  );
}
```

